# Lesson 10 – Publish an Agent and Configure Channels

## Overview

After designing, testing, and enhancing your agent with knowledge sources, topics, adaptive cards, and agent flows, the final step is to publish it so users can access it in real environments such as Microsoft Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot.

Publishing makes the latest version of your agent available to end users. Until the agent is published, any new changes you make in Copilot Studio remain unavailable to users, even if the agent appears fully configured in the authoring environment.

This lesson explains why publishing matters, what happens during publishing, how to configure channels, and what to consider when making the agent available across Microsoft Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot.

---

## Objectives

This lesson covers:

- Why it is important to publish your agent
- What happens when you publish your agent
- How to add a channel such as Microsoft Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot
- How to add the agent in Microsoft Teams
- How to make the agent available in Microsoft Teams for your organization

---

## Why Publishing Matters

While building an agent in Copilot Studio, you may add or update:

- Instructions
- Knowledge sources
- Topics
- Adaptive cards
- Tools
- Agent flows
- Channel settings

These updates are not automatically available to end users.

Publishing is the step that pushes the latest tested version of the agent live. If you update the agent but do not publish it, users will continue interacting with the last published version instead of the latest one.

This means publishing is essential whenever you want users to receive:

- New capabilities
- Updated logic
- Modified responses
- New tools or workflows
- Channel configuration updates

---

## What Happens When You Publish

When you publish an agent:

- The latest saved configuration becomes live
- Updates are made available to connected channels
- Users begin interacting with the newly published version
- Existing channels reflect the latest published changes

Publishing does not create a new agent. It updates the live version of the current one.

Because of this, publishing should happen only after proper testing and validation.

---

## Important Note About Trial Environments

There has been a change to Copilot Studio trial environments that prevents publishing of agents.

If your agent is in a trial environment:

- You may be able to build and test it
- You may not be able to publish it

A paid environment is required for full publishing capability.

If your goal is only to complete learning exercises, publishing may not be mandatory, but in real implementations, it is a required step for release.

---

## What Are Channels?

Channels define where users can access and interact with your agent.

After publishing the agent, you can configure one or more channels so the agent can be used in different places. A channel determines the delivery experience for the user.

Examples of channels include chat experiences, websites, collaboration tools, and business portals.

Each channel may render content differently depending on its capabilities.

---

## Available Channel Options

Copilot Studio supports multiple channels for agent deployment.

Common channel options include:

- Microsoft Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot
- Demo website
- Custom website
- Mobile app
- SharePoint
- Facebook Messenger
- Power Pages
- Azure Bot Service channels such as Slack, Telegram, and SMS integrations

Each channel may require its own setup and authentication steps.

---

## Microsoft Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot

One of the most important channel options is Microsoft Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot.

This channel allows your agent to be available:

- In Microsoft Teams chats
- In Microsoft Teams meetings
- Inside Microsoft 365 Copilot experiences

This is especially useful for internal enterprise agents because users can interact with the agent directly in the tools they already use for work.

---

## How to Configure Channels

To configure a channel:

1. Open the agent in Copilot Studio
2. Go to the Channels tab
3. Select the channel you want to add
4. Complete any required setup or permissions
5. Publish the agent so the channel configuration becomes live

Channel configuration should always be followed by testing in the actual destination channel.

---

## Why Channel Testing Is Important

Different channels do not always behave the same way.

An agent response that looks correct on a website may appear differently in Teams or another supported channel. Some channels support richer UI capabilities than others.

Because of this, it is important to validate the user experience in every target channel before releasing the agent broadly.

---

## Channel Experience Differences

When publishing to multiple channels, it is important to understand that feature support varies.

### Customer Satisfaction Survey

- Website: Supported with Adaptive Cards
- Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot: Text-only
- Facebook: Text-only
- Dynamics Omnichannel for Customer Service: Text-only

### Multiple-Choice Options

- Website: Supported
- Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot: Supported up to six options as a hero card
- Facebook: Supported up to thirteen options
- Dynamics Omnichannel for Customer Service: Partially supported

### Markdown

- Website: Supported
- Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot: Partially supported
- Facebook: Partially supported
- Dynamics Omnichannel for Customer Service: Partially supported

### Welcome Message

- Website: Supported
- Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot: Supported
- Facebook: Not supported
- Dynamics Omnichannel for Customer Service: Supported for chat, but not for all channel types

### Did-You-Mean Suggestions

- Website: Supported
- Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot: Supported
- Facebook: Supported
- Dynamics Omnichannel for Customer Service: Supported for selected channels, though sometimes suggestions are presented as plain text lists

These differences affect how you design topics, adaptive cards, prompts, and user guidance.

---

## Practical Design Considerations

When building for multiple channels:

- Keep interactions clear and channel-friendly
- Test adaptive cards in the channels where they will be used
- Avoid relying on formatting that may not render consistently
- Validate multiple-choice experiences within channel limits
- Consider alternate logic when certain channels need different behavior

In some cases, channel-specific logic may be appropriate, especially when richer cards are supported in one channel but not another.

---

## Adding the Agent to Microsoft Teams

Once the Microsoft Teams channel is configured and the agent is published, the agent can be added to Teams so users can interact with it directly.

Depending on tenant configuration and policies, this may involve:

- Adding the agent manually in Teams
- Pinning the app
- Making it available to users through Teams app policies
- Sharing the agent with specific users or groups

Whether the agent is available to the whole organization depends on Microsoft Teams and tenant-level administration settings.

---

## Making the Agent Available Across the Organization

To make the agent available broadly in Microsoft Teams, organizations may need to:

- Configure Teams app availability
- Allow custom or uploaded apps if required
- Manage setup policies
- Control who can access or install the agent
- Validate licensing and environment requirements

This means publication alone may not be enough for organization-wide visibility. Deployment readiness also depends on tenant governance and Teams administration.

---

## Recommended Publishing Process

A good publishing workflow is:

1. Finalize agent changes
2. Test the agent thoroughly in Copilot Studio
3. Review topics, tools, and knowledge references
4. Confirm channel configuration
5. Publish the agent
6. Test in the target channels such as Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot
7. Validate user experience and permissions
8. Roll out more broadly once confirmed

---

## Key Takeaways

- Publishing is what makes the latest version of your agent live
- Unpublished changes are not visible to end users
- Channels determine where the agent can be accessed
- Microsoft Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot are primary enterprise deployment channels
- Different channels support different experiences and UI capabilities
- Testing in each target channel is essential before broad release
- Trial environments may not support publishing

